<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Book Design on Typophile Archive</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/book-design/</link><description>Recent content in Book Design on Typophile Archive</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Thu, 05 Feb 2015 15:25:00 +0100</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/tags/book-design/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Critique of book layout... subject economic philosophy</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/123121/</link><pubDate>Thu, 05 Feb 2015 15:25:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/123121/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;m getting to the final stages of writing a book on economic philosophy. The central thesis: human societies have experienced only three basic &amp;ldquo;fields&amp;rdquo; of socio-economic behavior: agriculture/mining, mass production, and services; mass human labor is no longer needed in these three fields; a new fourth economy is necessary.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am attempting to set the format and type in InDesign, and would appreciate a critique (knowing full well there will be a wide variety of opinions). My interest is to put out the best, most readable design possible.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Serif and Sans Combo for a Two-Column Science Book</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/120797/</link><pubDate>Thu, 23 Oct 2014 11:23:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/120797/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;m going thru my type library, intent on using types I&amp;rsquo;ve not used in, like, forever, if ever. Though I love Univers&amp;ndash;and I believe this book will have scientific formulae in it&amp;ndash;with an Old Style serif&amp;ndash;I also like the idea of trying something I&amp;rsquo;ve had around forever, but just never got around to using, Stone Serif and Stone Sans. But I&amp;rsquo;m concerned about the narrow width of the text column, a touch less than 20p. I&amp;rsquo;m figuring a generous leading, but would still like something that fits well, in a compact kind of way (but, of course, not a Times) without looking jammed in.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Closest match to this Trajan-like titling from 1922</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/104337/</link><pubDate>Wed, 03 Jul 2013 13:24:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/104337/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Hello, typophiles,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Can anyone help me find the closest match to this sample from 1922 (book cover for Oswald Spengler&amp;rsquo;s Decline of the West - looks like gold leaf or perhaps embroidery?)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It is a Trajan-style, however Trajan itself doesn&amp;rsquo;t match the serifs, which are almost half-serifs. Once I have a typeface that&amp;rsquo;s pretty darned close, I can make modifications like the swashes etc.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for any assistance you can provide!&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Contemporary Artists Series - Phaidon Press (Book Design)</title><link>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/102051/</link><pubDate>Fri, 05 Apr 2013 10:48:00 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://jolg42.github.io/typophile/102051/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Hi people,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anyone wants to give this a go?&lt;/p&gt;
